snapshot replays to `[]` and then accepts later deltas; pinned by the pre-feature snapshot test.'","Testing: 'a pre-feature `SNAPSHOT` (no `releases` key) followed by a `RELEASE_DELTA` yields the delta's names'","Approach step 7 relies on an asserted fact: 'a pre-feature snapshot keeps the `[]` default'"],"finding":"The AC 'A pre-feature snapshot replays to `[]` and then accepts later deltas' asserts two outcomes, but the only listed test (pre-feature SNAPSHOT followed by a RELEASE_DELTA yields the delta's names) checks just the second. Nothing pins that a pre-feature SNAPSHOT with no later delta yields `releases == []` rather than a missing key. That missing-key case is exactly what happens if process_snapshot replaces state instead of merging into it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f329ecb0c9541d93c","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing (reducer tests) / AC 'A pre-feature snapshot replays to `[]`'","norm_id":"nb0eb34c8a6fd21c5","priority":0.2444,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["process_snapshot assigns compiled_state wholesale: a snapshot-only ticket returns state without `releases`, yet the delta-after-snapshot test could still pass if the fold uses setdefault."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In test_reducer_release_delta.py, add `test_pre_feature_snapshot_alone_yields_empty_releases`: replay only a SNAPSHOT whose compiled_state lacks `releases` and assert `state['releases'] == []`.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4444,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify that the forged cache uses the ticket's real event file stats so only the version differs, and add a control assertion that the same forged entry keyed with the current version IS served (proving the fixture is a cache hit apart from the version).","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5b","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Testing: 'forge a `.cache.json` keyed with `_REDUCER_CACHE_VERSION = 8` holding a state without `releases` for a ticket that has a `RELEASE_DELTA` ... then `reduce_ticket` returns the delta's names.'","Approach step 6: 'The cache key is that version plus event file stats (`_cache.py:128`)'"],"finding":"The stale-cache fixture does not require the forged `.cache.json` to match the ticket's current event file stats. The cache key is the version plus event stats (`_cache.py:128`). If the forged entry's stats differ from the real ones, the cache misses regardless of version, and the test passes even if `_REDUCER_CACHE_VERSION` is never bumped.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f78b2d734752938b3","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing: stale cache bullet in tests/interfaces/store/test_release_delta_store.py","norm_id":"n1271f28cc504f88f","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An implementer forgets step 6; the forged cache has stale stats, so it misses anyway, and the AC 'A reducer cache written under version 8 is recomputed' goes green without proving anything."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Build the forged key with the same helper `_cache.py` uses, passing version 8 and the live event stats. Add a control case: forge with the current `_REDUCER_CACHE_VERSION` and assert the forged (releases-less) state is returned. That proves the version alone drives the recompute.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.1111,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"no","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State in step 7 and in the v6 entry of docs/event-schema.md how an older binary handles a new-binary SNAPSHOT whose compiled_state contains `releases` (tolerated or ignored), and confirm the pre-v6 ticket_state schema/consumers do not reject unknown keys.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5b","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["T4","T7"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.5533,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Step 7: 'compaction stores the reduced state (`compact_txn.py:688-705`) and `process_snapshot` restores every key'","Step 4: 'Add `\"releases\": []` to `make_initial_state`'","Step 9: 'a new event body type, so older clones preserve and ignore it, and the reconcile host is upgraded first.'"],"finding":"The forward-compatibility note covers only older clones meeting the new RELEASE_DELTA event ('older clones preserve and ignore it'). It does not cover older clones restoring a SNAPSHOT compacted by a new binary. Because `make_initial_state` now always includes `\"releases\": []` and compaction stores the full reduced state, every new-binary snapshot will carry a `releases` key that an old binary's process_snapshot will restore. The plan does not state that this is harmless, for example that the old ticket_state schema or type consumers tolerate unknown keys.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f530ac978771b3106","impact":0.44,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 7 (Snapshot) / step 9 (Docs v6 history entry)","norm_id":"n282ef23f02eb6d5d","priority":0.1467,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A new clone compacts a ticket and pushes; an older clone replays the SNAPSHOT, and the extra `releases` key reaches its show_ticket output or schema validation (e.g. if the old ticket_state schema sets additionalProperties: false)."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3333,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"incomplete_enumeration","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Add a reducer test asserting a pre-feature SNAPSHOT with no later events reduces to `releases == []`, alongside the existing snapshot-then-delta test.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","T9","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E5","E3"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.7056,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Testing: \"a pre-feature `SNAPSHOT` (no `releases` key) followed by a `RELEASE_DELTA` yields the delta's names\"","Approach step 7 relies on \"process_snapshot restores every key ... a pre-feature snapshot keeps the `[]` default\" but no test isolates the snapshot-only replay"],"finding":"One acceptance criterion claims two things: a pre-feature snapshot replays to `[]`, and it then accepts later deltas. The only test it names checks just the second. That test is \"a pre-feature SNAPSHOT (no releases key) followed by a RELEASE_DELTA yields the delta's names\". No test asserts that a snapshot alone yields `releases == []`. If process_snapshot replaced state wholesale, the key would be absent rather than `[]`, and the delta fold might still recreate it. The test would pass while the stated `[]` outcome is unproven.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fae20a7e34580949e","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria: \"A pre-feature snapshot replays to `[]` and then accepts later deltas; pinned by the pre-feature snapshot test.\"","norm_id":"n3af693d6edfbd894","priority":0.2444,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A ticket compacted before this change, with no later RELEASE_DELTA, is read by a sync child. If the snapshot path drops the default, `state['releases']` raises KeyError or comes back missing from show_ticket, and no test catches it."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In test_reducer_release_delta.py, add a case: a CREATE plus a pre-feature SNAPSHOT whose compiled_state has no `releases` key, with no further events, gives `reduce_ticket(...)['releases'] == []`. Cite it in the AC line.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.4444,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"no"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"underspecified_certification","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State in step 9's v6 history entry that compaction on an un-upgraded clone can bury a RELEASE_DELTA behind its SNAPSHOT, and that a v6 `fsck` repair recovers it through _AUTO_RECOVER_ORPHAN_TYPES; add a store test that compacts with RELEASE_DELTA masked out of KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES, then shows the event flagged as an orphan and `releases` restored after repair.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","decision_margin":-0.4778,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan step 9: 'a new event body type, so older clones preserve and ignore it, and the reconcile host is upgraded first.'","src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py:268: unknown types are dropped from the fold candidates","src/rebar/_commands/compact_txn.py:337: snapshot_ts = (max(old_ts) if old_ts else now) + 1","src/rebar/reducer/_replay.py:138,159-160: replay starts at latest_snapshot_idx and `if idx < start_idx: continue`","src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py:58-76: an orphan must have a type in KNOWN_EVENT_TYPES and sort before the snapshot, so only a v6 binary flags a buried RELEASE_DELTA","Upgrading the reconcile host first controls who writes the events, not which clone compacts."],"finding":"The mixed-version case is incomplete. An un-upgraded (v5) clone that compacts a ticket leaves the unknown RELEASE_DELTA out of the fold, but dates the new SNAPSHOT at max(folded timestamps)+1. Any preserved RELEASE_DELTA older than that sorts before the SNAPSHOT. Upgraded readers then skip it, because replay starts at the latest SNAPSHOT index, so `releases` silently loses the delta. Recovery depends entirely on a v6 `fsck` repair: is_snapshot_orphan flags the event as an orphan and the auto-recover rebuild restores it. The plan describes the rollout only as 'older clones preserve and ignore it' and has no test for this path.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fd340ac51003d6564","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 5 (fsck auto-recover) and step 9 (v6 history entry); Testing → tests/interfaces/store/test_release_delta_store.py","norm_id":"nf4e19e0a351b20ad","priority":0.1222,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A developer clone on v5 runs compaction (sweep or per-close trigger) on a ticket after the v6 reconciler wrote a RELEASE_DELTA. The upgraded reconciler then reduces the ticket without that release and the outbound sync works from an incomplete list until someone runs fsck repair."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"In tests/interfaces/store/test_release_delta_store.py, add a case that: (1) appends a RELEASE_DELTA and a later known event; (2) runs `rebar store compact` with RELEASE_DELTA monkeypatche